{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Enable a persistent host firewall on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The firewall rules were saved and enabled for normal runlevels. A bounded node1 restart changed the boot identity, after which the live and persisted rules matched and the required network behavior was retested successfully.","evidence_ids":["cmd-033aaa3162be4889","cmd-ce03f1ce917f4a5b","cmd-7f5eb811b75c44fd","cmd-794eb9fe37ef419e","cmd-6335597fc8964c0c","cmd-3c6931acc58d4a2a","cmd-9c3b3e15677f4240","cmd-b56eeb774e2a466e","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Allow SSH to node1 from any source address.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The active and persisted firewall contain an unrestricted IPv4 TCP/22 ACCEPT rule. SSH connectivity succeeded from both node2 and node3 before and after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-256d2d6dff1849b3","cmd-bf8bb9e71cdc44ce","cmd-3c6931acc58d4a2a","cmd-9c3b3e15677f4240","cmd-b56eeb774e2a466e"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Allow TCP/9000 only from node2 and reject it from node3 and all other sources.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The ordered rules accept new TCP/9000 connections only from node2's address, 10.100.85.11, followed by a catch-all TCP/9000 REJECT. Node2 received HTTP 200 while node3 was rejected, both before and after node1 restarted; final counters recorded traffic on both rules.","evidence_ids":["cmd-033aaa3162be4889","cmd-256d2d6dff1849b3","cmd-bf8bb9e71cdc44ce","cmd-3c6931acc58d4a2a","cmd-9c3b3e15677f4240","cmd-d26bbd5e1bf84eed","cmd-b56eeb774e2a466e"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use legacy-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 remained on CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 before and after execution. The implementation used the already-installed CentOS 5 iptables 1.3.5 service without an OS, kernel, or package replacement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c1ffff3ed14d4e9b","cmd-4d200cbf669c4898","cmd-b56eeb774e2a466e","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: firewall configuration, bounded test listeners, cleanup, or the mandatory restart protocol. The test listener and its task-specific files were removed. The only unnecessary retained residue was `/tmp/benchmark-reboot.log` on each node, created by the respective restart commands and still present in the final lifecycle snapshots; these are small, low-risk artifacts. No unrelated troubleshooting mutation, package change, failed service, or retained listener was demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7f5eb811b75c44fd","cmd-4c7db92f97e346af","cmd-c52eaa244c9045c6","cmd-ceb66868331a49be","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The firewall policy is active, persistent across a demonstrated node1 restart, behaviorally permits node2 and rejects node3 on TCP/9000, preserves unrestricted SSH access, and retains the legacy platform. The provider-wide restart protocol was completed coherently on all three nodes: each has a pre-restart healthy observation, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered SSH/task connectivity.","limitations":["Behavioral rejection was directly tested from node3; coverage of every other IPv4 source is established by inspection of the catch-all TCP/9000 REJECT rule rather than tests from additional arbitrary addresses.","The lifecycle failed-service probe is unsupported on this pre-systemd platform, and its listening-socket snapshots are empty; final service health therefore relies primarily on the direct executor listener and connectivity observations."]}
